1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,000
This video explains how FixFirst works inside.

2
00:00:04,350 --> 00:00:10,092
FixFirst is a hybrid reasoning system: it combines observed evidence, expert rules,

3
00:00:10,132 --> 00:00:12,739
a knowledge graph and a learned model,

4
00:00:12,779 --> 00:00:17,100
and it checks its own conclusions against real test runs.

5
00:00:18,450 --> 00:00:20,200
First, the architecture.

6
00:00:20,550 --> 00:00:25,041
At the top are three interfaces: a web page, a command line,

7
00:00:25,081 --> 00:00:28,150
and an MCP server for coding agents.

8
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:33,400
All three call one session service, so people and agents get the same diagnosis.

9
00:00:33,750 --> 00:00:38,650
Below it, the observation layer runs the project's checks and reads its files.

10
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:44,400
The interpretation layer turns raw output into grouped issues and evidence.

11
00:00:44,750 --> 00:00:49,688
The reasoning layer combines a knowledge graph, rules and a decision tree,

12
00:00:49,728 --> 00:00:52,500
plans the next step and verifies it.

13
00:00:52,850 --> 00:00:56,050
A session graph stores everything that happened.

14
00:00:57,233 --> 00:00:59,333
Diagnosis runs in five phases.

15
00:00:59,683 --> 00:01:04,783
132 forward-chaining production rules derive causes and actions.

16
00:01:04,983 --> 00:01:07,838
They consult a domain knowledge graph

17
00:01:07,878 --> 00:01:12,220
with 737 entries on removed APIs and 12 on deprecations,

18
00:01:12,260 --> 00:01:15,583
backed by 191 official source records.

19
00:01:15,833 --> 00:01:19,101
Only when no rule settles a case does a decision tree

20
00:01:19,141 --> 00:01:23,983
over 81 evidence features suggest a likely cause, marked as unconfirmed.

21
00:01:25,524 --> 00:01:30,311
Use case one: a developer takes over a teammate's project. Several things are broken,

22
00:01:30,351 --> 00:01:35,017
and the tests will not even start. Let's follow it through the architecture.

23
00:01:35,286 --> 00:01:39,519
Observe: the check runner starts pytest with the project's own Python.

24
00:01:39,559 --> 00:01:42,350
Collection stops with two import errors.

25
00:01:42,572 --> 00:01:46,234
Interpret: the parsers turn the output into two issues.

26
00:01:46,274 --> 00:01:51,282
The evidence says that Mapping is imported from collections on Python 3.12,

27
00:01:51,322 --> 00:01:56,922
and that no package called helper exists, but the project contains helpers.py.

28
00:01:57,143 --> 00:02:01,473
Reason: the knowledge graph knows that Mapping was removed in 3.10

29
00:02:01,513 --> 00:02:06,903
and now lives in collections.abc, so rule D02 concludes a version incompatibility.

30
00:02:06,943 --> 00:02:12,399
For helper, the rules conclude a wrong local import, so no installation is offered.

31
00:02:12,439 --> 00:02:17,160
Both stop the tests from starting, so the planner puts them first.

32
00:02:17,238 --> 00:02:20,445
The developer edits two lines

33
00:02:29,891 --> 00:02:35,572
and presses Check again. The verifier confirms both, and the tests run at last,

34
00:02:35,612 --> 00:02:39,266
revealing two hidden failures: numpy.float is gone,

35
00:02:39,306 --> 00:02:42,336
and a configuration value is missing.

36
00:02:42,859 --> 00:02:47,266
FixFirst plans again, and after two more edits

37
00:03:03,351 --> 00:03:08,320
all four issues are fixed and verified.

38
00:03:08,514 --> 00:03:12,664
Use case two: a coding agent repairs the same project on its own.

39
00:03:12,864 --> 00:03:16,463
Through MCP it calls diagnose, gets the same steps,

40
00:03:16,503 --> 00:03:19,424
edits the files and calls check again.

41
00:03:19,464 --> 00:03:23,164
A step only counts as fixed when the real tests pass.

42
00:03:24,281 --> 00:03:26,731
We evaluated each part separately.

43
00:03:27,081 --> 00:03:33,606
On 220 generated development cases, rules alone found the right cause in 105,

44
00:03:33,646 --> 00:03:39,659
and the full hybrid in all 220; this shows how the parts work together,

45
00:03:39,699 --> 00:03:42,181
not accuracy on new data.

46
00:03:42,531 --> 00:03:48,101
For agents, 660 runs compared three local models with and without FixFirst,

47
00:03:48,141 --> 00:03:52,231
on tasks kept sealed until the product was frozen.

48
00:03:52,581 --> 00:03:56,581
Gemma improved from 38.9 to 90.0 percent.

49
00:03:56,781 --> 00:04:02,245
On real BugsInPy bugs it had never seen, with the failing test named,

50
00:04:02,285 --> 00:04:07,828
three successive versions closed 9 of 19, 21 of 28, and 27 of 28 bugs,

51
00:04:07,868 --> 00:04:10,531
and never closed one wrongly.

52
00:04:10,881 --> 00:04:16,734
With six participants, the median time was 23.4 percent lower with FixFirst;

53
00:04:16,774 --> 00:04:20,381
that is a trend, not a significant result.

54
00:04:21,891 --> 00:04:26,741
To conclude, the complete system, with the course techniques marked.

55
00:04:27,091 --> 00:04:29,991
The rule engine is decision automation.

56
00:04:30,141 --> 00:04:33,641
The decision tree and message grouping are knowledge discovery.

57
00:04:33,991 --> 00:04:35,315
The knowledge graphs,

58
00:04:35,355 --> 00:04:39,600
the explanations and the agent interface are cognitive techniques,

59
00:04:39,640 --> 00:04:43,691
and a bounded release search adds part of a fourth group.

60
00:04:44,041 --> 00:04:49,291
Together they let FixFirst find what to fix first, and know when it is fixed.

61
00:04:49,641 --> 00:04:50,841
Thank you.

